.SH "Enumeration Type Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiBlendMode\fP"

.PP
Defines alpha-blend flags\&. If you're familiar with OpenGL or D3D, these flags aren't new to you\&. They define \fIhow\fP the final color value of a pixel is computed, basing on the previous color at that pixel and the new color value from the material\&. The blend formula is: 
.PP
.nf
SourceColor * SourceBlend + DestColor * DestBlend

.fi
.PP
 where DestColor is the previous color in the frame-buffer at this position and SourceColor is the material color before the transparency calculation\&.
.br
 This corresponds to the \fBAI_MATKEY_BLEND_FUNC\fP property\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiBlendMode_Default \fP\fP
Formula: 
.PP
.nf
SourceColor*SourceAlpha + DestColor*(1\-SourceAlpha)

.fi
.PP
 
.TP
\fB\fIaiBlendMode_Additive \fP\fP
Additive blending
.PP
Formula: 
.PP
.nf
SourceColor*1 + DestColor*1

.fi
.PP
 
.TP
\fB\fI_aiBlendMode_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiShadingMode\fP"

.PP
Defines all shading models supported by the library\&. Property: \fBAI_MATKEY_SHADING_MODEL\fP
.PP
The list of shading modes has been taken from Blender\&. See Blender documentation for more information\&. The API does not distinguish between 'specular' and 'diffuse' shaders (thus the specular term for diffuse shading models like Oren-Nayar remains undefined)\&. 
.br
 Again, this value is just a hint\&. \fBAssimp\fP tries to select the shader whose most common implementation matches the original rendering results of the 3D modeler which wrote a particular model as closely as possible\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Flat \fP\fP
Flat shading\&. Shading is done on per-face base, diffuse only\&. Also known as 'faceted shading'\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Gouraud \fP\fP
Simple Gouraud shading\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Phong \fP\fP
Phong-Shading - 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Blinn \fP\fP
Phong-Blinn-Shading 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Toon \fP\fP
Toon-Shading per pixel
.PP
Also known as 'comic' shader\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_OrenNayar \fP\fP
OrenNayar-Shading per pixel
.PP
Extension to standard Lambertian shading, taking the roughness of the material into account 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Minnaert \fP\fP
Minnaert-Shading per pixel
.PP
Extension to standard Lambertian shading, taking the 'darkness' of the material into account 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_CookTorrance \fP\fP
CookTorrance-Shading per pixel
.PP
Special shader for metallic surfaces\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_NoShading \fP\fP
No shading at all\&. Constant light influence of 1\&.0\&. Also known as 'Unlit' 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Unlit \fP\fP
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_Fresnel \fP\fP
Fresnel shading 
.TP
\fB\fIaiShadingMode_PBR_BRDF \fP\fP
Physically-Based Rendering (PBR) shading using Bidirectional scattering/reflectance distribution function (BSDF/BRDF) There are multiple methods under this banner, and model files may provide data for more than one PBR-BRDF method\&. Applications should use the set of provided properties to determine which of their preferred PBR rendering methods are likely to be available eg:
.IP "\(bu" 2
If AI_MATKEY_METALLIC_FACTOR is set, then a Metallic/Roughness is available
.IP "\(bu" 2
If AI_MATKEY_GLOSSINESS_FACTOR is set, then a Specular/Glossiness is available Note that some PBR methods allow layering of techniques 
.PP

.TP
\fB\fI_aiShadingMode_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiTextureFlags\fP"

.PP
Defines some mixed flags for a particular texture\&. Usually you'll instruct your cg artists how textures have to look like \&.\&.\&. and how they will be processed in your application\&. However, if you use \fBAssimp\fP for completely generic loading purposes you might also need to process these flags in order to display as many 'unknown' 3D models as possible correctly\&.
.PP
This corresponds to the \fBAI_MATKEY_TEXFLAGS\fP property\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureFlags_Invert \fP\fP
The texture's color values have to be inverted (component-wise 1-n) 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureFlags_UseAlpha \fP\fP
Explicit request to the application to process the alpha channel of the texture\&.
.PP
Mutually exclusive with \fBaiTextureFlags_IgnoreAlpha\fP\&. These flags are set if the library can say for sure that the alpha channel is used/is not used\&. If the model format does not define this, it is left to the application to decide whether the texture alpha channel - if any - is evaluated or not\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureFlags_IgnoreAlpha \fP\fP
Explicit request to the application to ignore the alpha channel of the texture\&.
.PP
Mutually exclusive with \fBaiTextureFlags_UseAlpha\fP\&. 
.TP
\fB\fI_aiTextureFlags_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiTextureMapMode\fP"

.PP
Defines how UV coordinates outside the [0\&.\&.\&.1] range are handled\&. Commonly referred to as 'wrapping mode'\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapMode_Wrap \fP\fP
A texture coordinate u|v is translated to u%1|v%1 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapMode_Clamp \fP\fP
Texture coordinates outside [0\&.\&.\&.1] are clamped to the nearest valid value\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapMode_Decal \fP\fP
If the texture coordinates for a pixel are outside [0\&.\&.\&.1] the texture is not applied to that pixel 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapMode_Mirror \fP\fP
A texture coordinate u|v becomes u%1|v%1 if (u-(u%1))%2 is zero and 1-(u%1)|1-(v%1) otherwise 
.TP
\fB\fI_aiTextureMapMode_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiTextureMapping\fP"

.PP
Defines how the mapping coords for a texture are generated\&. Real-time applications typically require full UV coordinates, so the use of the aiProcess_GenUVCoords step is highly recommended\&. It generates proper UV channels for non-UV mapped objects, as long as an accurate description how the mapping should look like (e\&.g spherical) is given\&. See the \fBAI_MATKEY_MAPPING\fP property for more details\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapping_UV \fP\fP
The mapping coordinates are taken from an UV channel\&.
.PP
\fBAI_MATKEY_UVWSRC\fP property specifies from which UV channel the texture coordinates are to be taken from (remember, meshes can have more than one UV channel)\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapping_SPHERE \fP\fP
Spherical mapping 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapping_CYLINDER \fP\fP
Cylindrical mapping 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapping_BOX \fP\fP
Cubic mapping 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapping_PLANE \fP\fP
Planar mapping 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureMapping_OTHER \fP\fP
Undefined mapping\&. Have fun\&. 
.TP
\fB\fI_aiTextureMapping_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiTextureOp\fP"

.PP
Defines how the Nth texture of a specific type is combined with the result of all previous layers\&. Example (left: key, right: value): 
.br
 
.PP
.nf
DiffColor0     \- gray
DiffTextureOp0 \- aiTextureOpMultiply
DiffTexture0   \- tex1\&.png
DiffTextureOp0 \- aiTextureOpAdd
DiffTexture1   \- tex2\&.png

.fi
.PP
 Written as equation, the final diffuse term for a specific pixel would be: 
.PP
.nf
diffFinal = DiffColor0 * sampleTex(DiffTexture0,UV0) +
   sampleTex(DiffTexture1,UV0) * diffContrib;

.fi
.PP
 where 'diffContrib' is the intensity of the incoming light for that pixel\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureOp_Multiply \fP\fP
T = T1 * T2 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureOp_Add \fP\fP
T = T1 + T2 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureOp_Subtract \fP\fP
T = T1 - T2 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureOp_Divide \fP\fP
T = T1 / T2 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureOp_SmoothAdd \fP\fP
T = (T1 + T2) - (T1 * T2) 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureOp_SignedAdd \fP\fP
T = T1 + (T2-0\&.5) 
.TP
\fB\fI_aiTextureOp_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SS "\fBenum\fP \fBaiTextureType\fP"

.PP
Defines the purpose of a texture\&. This is a very difficult topic\&. Different 3D packages support different kinds of textures\&. For very common texture types, such as bumpmaps, the rendering results depend on implementation details in the rendering pipelines of these applications\&. \fBAssimp\fP loads all texture references from the model file and tries to determine which of the predefined texture types below is the best choice to match the original use of the texture as closely as possible\&.
.br
.PP
In content pipelines you'll usually define how textures have to be handled, and the artists working on models have to conform to this specification, regardless which 3D tool they're using\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_NONE \fP\fP
Dummy value\&.
.PP
No texture, but the value to be used as 'texture semantic' (#aiMaterialProperty::mSemantic) for all material properties \fInot\fP related to textures\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_DIFFUSE \fP\fP
LEGACY API MATERIALS Legacy refers to materials which Were originally implemented in the specifications around 2000\&. These must never be removed, as most engines support them\&. The texture is combined with the result of the diffuse lighting equation\&. OR PBR Specular/Glossiness 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_SPECULAR \fP\fP
The texture is combined with the result of the specular lighting equation\&. OR PBR Specular/Glossiness 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_AMBIENT \fP\fP
The texture is combined with the result of the ambient lighting equation\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_EMISSIVE \fP\fP
The texture is added to the result of the lighting calculation\&. It isn't influenced by incoming light\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_HEIGHT \fP\fP
The texture is a height map\&.
.PP
By convention, higher gray-scale values stand for higher elevations from the base height\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_NORMALS \fP\fP
The texture is a (tangent space) normal-map\&.
.PP
Again, there are several conventions for tangent-space normal maps\&. \fBAssimp\fP does (intentionally) not distinguish here\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_SHININESS \fP\fP
The texture defines the glossiness of the material\&.
.PP
The glossiness is in fact the exponent of the specular (phong) lighting equation\&. Usually there is a conversion function defined to map the linear color values in the texture to a suitable exponent\&. Have fun\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_OPACITY \fP\fP
The texture defines per-pixel opacity\&.
.PP
Usually 'white' means opaque and 'black' means 'transparency'\&. Or quite the opposite\&. Have fun\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_DISPLACEMENT \fP\fP
Displacement texture
.PP
The exact purpose and format is application-dependent\&. Higher color values stand for higher vertex displacements\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_LIGHTMAP \fP\fP
Lightmap texture (aka Ambient Occlusion)
.PP
Both 'Lightmaps' and dedicated 'ambient occlusion maps' are covered by this material property\&. The texture contains a scaling value for the final color value of a pixel\&. Its intensity is not affected by incoming light\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_REFLECTION \fP\fP
Reflection texture
.PP
Contains the color of a perfect mirror reflection\&. Rarely used, almost never for real-time applications\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_BASE_COLOR \fP\fP
PBR Materials PBR definitions from maya and other modelling packages now use this standard\&. This was originally introduced around 2012\&. Support for this is in game engines like Godot, Unreal or Unity3D\&. Modelling packages which use this are very common now\&. 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_NORMAL_CAMERA \fP\fP
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_EMISSION_COLOR \fP\fP
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_METALNESS \fP\fP
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_DIFFUSE_ROUGHNESS \fP\fP
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_AMBIENT_OCCLUSION \fP\fP
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_SHEEN \fP\fP
PBR \fBMaterial\fP Modifiers Some modern renderers have further PBR modifiers that may be overlaid on top of the 'base' PBR materials for additional realism\&. These use multiple texture maps, so only the base type is directly defined Sheen Generally used to simulate textiles that are covered in a layer of microfibers eg velvet https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF/tree/master/extensions/2.0/Khronos/KHR_materials_sheen 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_CLEARCOAT \fP\fP
Clearcoat Simulates a layer of 'polish' or 'laquer' layered on top of a PBR substrate https://autodesk.github.io/standard-surface/#closures/coating https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF/tree/master/extensions/2.0/Khronos/KHR_materials_clearcoat 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_TRANSMISSION \fP\fP
Transmission Simulates transmission through the surface May include further information such as wall thickness 
.TP
\fB\fIaiTextureType_UNKNOWN \fP\fP
Unknown texture
.PP
A texture reference that does not match any of the definitions above is considered to be 'unknown'\&. It is still imported, but is excluded from any further post-processing\&. 
.TP
\fB\fI_aiTextureType_Force32Bit \fP\fP
.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "\fBASSIMP_API\fP \fBC_ENUM\fP \fBaiReturn\fP aiGetMaterialColor (const \fBC_STRUCT\fP \fBaiMaterial\fP * pMat, const char * pKey, unsigned int type, unsigned int index, \fBC_STRUCT\fP \fBaiColor4D\fP * pOut)"

.PP
Retrieve a color value from the material property table\&. See the sample for aiGetMaterialFloat for more information 
.SS "\fBaiReturn\fP aiGetMaterialFloat (const \fBC_STRUCT\fP \fBaiMaterial\fP * pMat, const char * pKey, unsigned int type, unsigned int index, \fBai_real\fP * pOut)\fR [inline]\fP"

.PP
Retrieve a single float property with a specific key from the material\&. Pass one of the AI_MATKEY_XXX constants for the last three parameters (the example reads the \fBAI_MATKEY_SHININESS_STRENGTH\fP property of the first diffuse texture) 
.PP
.nf
float specStrength = 1\&.f; // default value, remains unmodified if we fail\&.
aiGetMaterialFloat(mat, AI_MATKEY_SHININESS_STRENGTH,
   (float*)&specStrength);

.fi
.PP
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIpMat\fP Pointer to the input material\&. May not be NULL 
.br
\fIpKey\fP Key to search for\&. One of the AI_MATKEY_XXX constants\&. 
.br
\fIpOut\fP Receives the output float\&. 
.br
\fItype\fP (see the code sample above) 
.br
\fIindex\fP (see the code sample above) 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Specifies whether the key has been found\&. If not, the output float remains unmodified\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBASSIMP_API\fP \fBC_ENUM\fP \fBaiReturn\fP aiGetMaterialFloatArray (const \fBC_STRUCT\fP \fBaiMaterial\fP * pMat, const char * pKey, unsigned int type, unsigned int index, \fBai_real\fP * pOut, unsigned int * pMax)"

.PP
Retrieve an array of float values with a specific key from the material\&. Pass one of the AI_MATKEY_XXX constants for the last three parameters (the example reads the \fBAI_MATKEY_UVTRANSFORM\fP property of the first diffuse texture) 
.PP
.nf
aiUVTransform trafo;
unsigned int max = sizeof(aiUVTransform);
if (AI_SUCCESS != aiGetMaterialFloatArray(mat, AI_MATKEY_UVTRANSFORM(aiTextureType_DIFFUSE,0),
   (float*)&trafo, &max) || sizeof(aiUVTransform) != max)
{
  // error handling
}

.fi
.PP
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIpMat\fP Pointer to the input material\&. May not be NULL 
.br
\fIpKey\fP Key to search for\&. One of the AI_MATKEY_XXX constants\&. 
.br
\fIpOut\fP Pointer to a buffer to receive the result\&. 
.br
\fIpMax\fP Specifies the size of the given buffer, in float's\&. Receives the number of values (not bytes!) read\&. 
.br
\fItype\fP (see the code sample above) 
.br
\fIindex\fP (see the code sample above) 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Specifies whether the key has been found\&. If not, the output arrays remains unmodified and pMax is set to 0\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBaiReturn\fP aiGetMaterialInteger (const \fBC_STRUCT\fP \fBaiMaterial\fP * pMat, const char * pKey, unsigned int type, unsigned int index, int * pOut)\fR [inline]\fP"

